      "text": "To find the inverse of the function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to apply the following steps:\n\n1. **Identify the individual functions and their inverses:**\n   - \\( p(x) \\) can be inverted to get \\( p^{-1}(x) \\) because \\( p \\) is invertible.\n   - \\( q(x) \\) can be inverted to get \\( q^{-1}(x) \\) because \\( q \\) is invertible.\n   - For \\( r(x) \\), the inverse is \\( r^{-1}(x) \\) because \\( r \\) is invertible.\n\n2. **Understand the composition of functions:**\n   - The composition of functions \\( p \\circ r \\circ q \\) means first apply \\( r \\), then \\( q \\), and finally \\( p \\).\n   - If we apply the inverse functions in the reverse order (from right to left), we get \\( p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\).\n\n3. **Verify the order of the inverses:**\n   - Theorem: If \\( g_1(x), g_2(x), \\ldots, g_n(x) \\) are invertible functions, then the inverse of the composition \\( g_n \\circ g_{n-1} \\circ \\ldots \\circ g_1(x) \\) is \\( g_1^{-1} \\circ g_2^{-1} \\circ \\ldots \\circ g_n^{-1} \\).\n\nTherefore, for our function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), the inverse \\( f^{-1} \\) should be \\( p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\).\n\nThus, the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]",
