      "text": "To find the inverse of the function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to apply the inverse operations in reverse order. This is because the function \\( f \\) is a composition of \\( q \\), \\( p \\), and \\( r \\), and the inverse function will also be composed of \\( q \\), \\( p \\), and \\( r \\) in the reverse order, but in reverse steps.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Start with the function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\).\n2. To find the inverse \\( f^{-1} \\), we need to reverse the composition. So, we first inverse the composition \\( r \\circ p \\circ q \\):\n   - The inverse of \\( r \\circ p \\circ q \\) is \\( q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\).\n   - To see why, let's denote the composition \\( r \\circ p \\circ q \\) by \\( g \\), so \\( g = r \\circ p \\circ q \\). Taking the inverse of \\( g \\) gives us \\( g^{-1} \\). Now, if \\( g = r \\circ p \\circ q \\), then \\( g^{-1} = q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\). So, \\( g^{-1} = f^{-1} \\).\n\nThus, the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n\\[ f^{-1} = q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\]\n\nThe letter corresponding to this expression is \\( \\boxed{D} \\).",
